Basic Hacking Tools:
====================
Reconnaissance phase:
---------------------
        Involves gathering information about a target (individual, organization, or network) to identify potential vulnerabilities and determine the best approach for an attack.
        It is the first step in the hacking lifecycle and is often known as Information Gathering.

Passive Reconnaissance vs Active Reconnaissance:-
-------------------------------------------------
Passive Reconnaissance:
=> Gathering publicly available information without interacting directly with the target
=> Examples: WHOIS lookups, searching company websites, social media, or public forums, Analyzing DNS records and metadata from public documents.

Active Reconnaissance:
=> Direct interaction with the target's systems to gather information.
=> Examples: Scanning networks and ports, DNS queries, Enumerating services and users.

Purpose of Active Reconnaissance:
----------------------------------
=> Gather precise and actionable data about a target system or network.
=> Identify potential entry points and vulnerabilities to inform further phases like exploitation.
=> Move from general knowledge (Passive reconnaissance) to specific technical insights.
=> Helps identify live hosts, services, open ports, and configurations.
=> Informs the tools and methods to use in later phases.
=> Reduces unnecessary scanning by focusing on known target characteristics.

Direct interaction with the target provides real-time, accurate information, enables strategy refinement for subsequent attack phases, and lays the foundation for discovering exploitable vulnerabilities.

Reconnaissance (active & passive) is considered cyclic because it involves an iterative process of gathering, analyzing, and refining information to develop a comprehensive understanding of the target system 

Note: Active reconnaissance can alert the target to your presence. Always assess the risk of detection before employing these techniques.

Active DNS enumeration tools:
------------------------------
Focuses on identifying and analyzing the Domain Name System (DNS) records and infrastructure of a target organization.
Actively querying DNS reveals:-
=> Hostnames (eg: mail.example.com, vpn.example.com)
=> Associated IP address
=> Subdomains, which may expose staging environments, admin panels, or other sensitive services.

DNS misconfigurations can expose critical information, such as:
=> Internal records mistakenly exposed to the public
=> Misaligned or outdated records pointing to decommissioned infrastructure.
=> Unprotected subdomains vulnerable to takeover.

Each DNS record potentially points to a service or system that can be exploited. For example:
=> An MX (Mail Exchange) record might lead to an email server.
=> A TXT record might reveal SPF, DKIM, or DMARC configurations useful for phishing.

DNS information can aid in launching targeted attacks:-
=> Crafting phishing campaigns using valid subdomains
=> Identifying exploitable vulnerabilities in specific services.
DNS records like SRV (Service) or CNAME (Canonical Name) provide insights into backend systems and configurations.

Subdomains:
-----------
=> Subdivisions of a primary domain.
=> Represent distinct sections of a website or service under the same domain. Examples: blog.example.com, admin.example.com
=> Serve different functionalities like organizing content/services (eg: public website vs internal admin portal)

Virtual Hosts (VHOSTS):
-----------------------
=> Allows multiple websites or services to run on the same server, distinguished by their domain or IP address. Example: example.com, anotherexample.com
=> Types of VHOSTS: Name-Based VHOSTS - Distinguish sites by domain name; IP-Based VHOSTS - Use separate IPs for each site.

Importance of subdomains:
--------------------------
=> Reconnaissance:
   -> Discover hidden services (admin, dev, staging)
   -> Identify less-secured environments (eg: forgotten test subdomains)
=> Attack Surface Expansion:
   -> Each subdomain may run distinct software, increasing attack opportunities.
   -> Example: An outdated CMS on blog.example.com
=> Discovery tools: Tools like Amass and Fierce can enumerate subdomains

Importance of VHOSTS:
---------------------
=> Discovering Hidden VHOSTS:
    -> Some servers host services not listed in DNS, accessible only via Host header manipulation.
    -> Example: Host: secret.example.com reveals a hidden service
=> Exploiting misconfigurations: 
    -> Poorly secured VHOSTS might expose sensitive data.
    -> Example: An admin panel on a forgotten VHOST.
=> Discovery tools: Burp Suite, Gobuster, or custom scripts with header-based fuzzing.
Discovering VHOST is more difficult than subdomains and usually needs to be brute-forced.

ASN Numbers:
------------
Autonomous System Number (ASN) is a unique identifier assigned to an Autonomous System (AS), which is a group of IP networks managed by a single organization, such as an ISP or enterprise.
Enables different networks to exchange routing information on the global internet via BGP (Border Gateway Protocol)

Working of ASN:
---------------
Autonomous Systems (AS) are responsible for routing traffic.

Role of ASN in Routing:
=> ASN identifies the AS during internet traffic exchanges.
=> Helps BGP determine the best path for data.

Types of ASN:
=> Public ASN: For networks interacting with the internet.
=> Private ASN: For internal routing, not visible on the internet.

Example: Traffic from your device to a website crosses multiple ASes, each identified by its ASN.

DNS Zone Transfers:
-------------------
Mechanism used to replicate the data from one DNS server to another, typically from a primary (master) DNS server to a secondary (slave) DNS server. It ensures redundancy and consistency of DNS records within a network.

Types of Zone transfers:
=> Full Zone Transfer (AXFR): Transfers the entire DNS zone.
=> Incremental Zone Transfer (IXFR): Transfers only the changes since the last update, making it more efficient.

Data contained in a zone transfer:
=> A list of subdomains
=> Associated IP addresses (A/AAAA records)
=> Mail server records (MX records)
=> Other DNS configurations.

A successful DNS zone transfer can reveal sensitive information about a target's infrastructure, including:
=> Internal server names
=> Subdomains not meant to be public
=> Mail servers, potentially useful for phishing
=> Connections between systems.
=> This can act as a blueprint of the target's network.

DNS Querying Tools:
--------------------
=> nslookup:
   -> Basic DNS querying tool to query specific DNS record types (eg: A, MX, TXT)
   -> Available in Windows and Linux
   -> Used for finding Subdomains, new domains, IP addresses & IT infrastructure assets
   -> Example: nslookup -type=TXT example.com

=> host:
    -> Basic DNS querying tool to query specific DNS record types (eg: A, MX, TXT) (Has many similarities with the nslookup tool)
    -> Available in Linux
    -> Used for finding Subdomains, new domains, IP addresses & IT infrastructure assets.
    -> Example: host -t TXT example.com
    -> Note: We can use these commands to create a script that enumerates subdomains based on a specific input list of potential subdomains:
                          for ip in $(cat subbrute.txt); do host $ip.example.com; done
Wordlist for enumerating subdomains: https://github.com/theMiddleBlue/DNSenum/blob/master/wordlist/subdomains-top1mil-20000.txt

=> dig:
     -> Performs detailed DNS lookups and traces DNS paths.
     -> Available in Windows and Linux
     -> Used for finding Subdomains, new domains, IP addresses & IT infrastructure assets.
     -> Example: dig example.com ANY

=> dnsenum:
      -> Automates DNS enumeration for subdomains and records.
      -> Available in Windows and Linux
      -> Used for finding Subdomains, new domains, IP addresses, Zone transfers & IT infrastructure assets.
      -> Example: dnsenum example.com

=> fierce:
      -> DNS Reconnaissance focused on subdomain enumeration and detecting DNS misconfigurations
      -> Available in Windows and Linux
      -> Used for finding Subdomains, new domains, IP addresses & IT infrastructure assets.
      -> Example: fierce --domain example.com

=> aiodnsbrute:
      -> DNS reconnaissance focused on subdomain enumeration with built-in lists and fast results on the CLI. URL:https://github.com/blark/aiodnsbrute
      -> Available in Windows and Linux
      -> Used for finding subdomains and new domains
      -> Example: aiodnsbrute example.com

=> amass:
      -> Comprehensive tool for passive and active DNS enumeration.
      -> Available in Windows and Linux
      -> Used for enumerating subdomains using multiple sources, including brute-forcing.
      -> Example (subdomain enum): amass enum -d example.com

Note 1: You can also check all SSL certificates returned by the company's endpoint. Certificates usually contain interesting domain names.
Note 2: Always perform DNS enumeration from a VPS (Virtual Private Server / Hosted Server) or with a VPN. This will increase speed and avoid getting flagged or blocked.

VHOSTS enumeration:
--------------------
Brute-forcing VHOSTS in environments where virtual hosting is being used.

=> Gobuster:
gobuster vhost --useragent "Mozilla/5.0 (x11; Linux x86_64; rv:128.0) Gecko/20100101 firefox/128.0" --wordlist "/usr/share/wordlists/SecLists/Discovery/DNS/namelist.txt" --url example.com

